<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John D. Sloat</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1781">1781</a>–<a href="/years/1867">1867</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as John Drake Sloat</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Drake Sloat was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-navy">United States Navy</a> officer who commanded the Pacific Squadron at the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-american-war">Mexican–American War</a>. On 7 July 1846 he landed at Monterey and claimed Alta <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, ending the California Republic. He handed command to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-f-stockton">Robert F. Stockton</a> weeks later and eventually retired as a rear admiral.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-d-sloat.jpg" alt="John D. Sloat" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Edwin A. Sherman (author of The life of the late Rear-Admiral John Drake Sloat of the United States Navy, published 1902) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_D._Sloat.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>John Drake Sloat was born on 26 July 1781 at the family home, Sloat House, in Sloatsburg, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> ancestry. His father, Captain John Sloat, was shot by mistake by one of his own sentries two months before the boy's birth, and his mother died a few years later, leaving him an orphan. The town of Sloatsburg is named after his grandfather, Stephen Sloat.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Appointed a midshipman in 1800, Sloat was sailing master of the frigate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> under Commodore Stephen Decatur in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-1812">War of 1812</a> and was promoted to lieutenant for gallantry in the capture of HMS Macedonian. Commanding the schooner Grampus, he fought the campaign of March 1825 against the pirate Roberto Cofresí. He became a master commandant in 1826, commanded the sloop St. Louis from 1828, was promoted to captain in 1837 and ran the Portsmouth Navy Yard from 1840 to 1844.</p><p>Given the Pacific Squadron in 1844, he was ordered in 1845 to land in Alta California and claim it if war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> broke out. Hearing off Mazatlán of fighting on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> border, he sailed north, defeated Mexican forces at the Battle of Monterey, raised the flag over the Customs House on 7 July 1846 and proclaimed California part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. He was military governor for only twenty-two days before handing over to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-f-stockton">Robert F. Stockton</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Poor health then kept him to shore commands: the Norfolk Navy Yard from 1847 to 1851, the building of the Stevens Battery in 1855, and the board of 1852 that chose Mare Island as the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> naval base on the Pacific. He retired in December 1861 and was made rear admiral on the retired list in July 1866. He died at New Brighton, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, on 28 November 1867 and was buried in Green-Wood Cemetery in Brooklyn. Two destroyers named USS Sloat and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a>'s Sloat Boulevard honour him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John D. Sloat” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>During the Mexican–American War, Commodore <b>John Drake Sloat</b>'s ships captured Monterey, the Alta Californian capital, without firing a shot.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-7">7 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>On 7 July 1846 Commodore <b>John Sloat</b> raised the American flag at Monterey, and the Stars and Stripes replaced the Bear Flag at Sonoma on 9 July.</blockquote><a href="/state/california-republic">California Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Sloatsburg station, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in New Brighton, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.16,-74.19&amp;year=1836" aria-label="Sloatsburg station on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of John D. Sloat"><image href="/maps/globes/g300n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4096 3996h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4096 3996h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.16,-74.19&amp;year=1836">What was here in 1836? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
